I’ve great respect for Arvind Narayanan & understand why he is so skeptical of AI-doom forecasting.
I agree: Data to date are mostly consistent with hypothesis that p(doom) debates stretch subjective-probability forecasting to the limit.
If there’s disagreement, it is over whether AI-doom forecasting is 100% noise—a conclusion I’m reluctant to draw for 3 reasons:
First, there is still much room for improvement in the efforts to facilitate adversarial collaborations between (the most thoughtful) proponents of clashing schools of thought. Best studies to date are not good enough to sustain Arvind’s pessimistic conclusion;
Second, order of magnitude differences over p(doom 2100) shrunk when forecasters focused on well-chosen, early warning indicator questions in late 2020s, which suggests tournaments may yet be useful in accelerating Bayesian convergence on truth;
Third, asymmetric costs. Defunding promising forecasting efforts is, by a large margin, a more serious mistake than funding futile forecasting efforts.

