This essay is worth reading for its extraordinary depiction of the author's efforts to appease their audience while justifying their prejudice against LLMs, using only sonnet as an example. It highlights a trend where critics of AI not only ignore its capabilities but also attack those who acknowledge them, akin to ostriches burying their heads in the sand.

Four years ago, the argument was that AI ethics should be grounded in a deep understanding of technology, but now, those focused on immediate harms seem trapped in an epistemic blind alley, enforcing their views with moral criticism. The denialists should embrace the reality of using transformative technology without guilt and redirect their talents towards positive contributions, such as developing open models.

@mmitchell_ai: "Strikes me as a key research direction for people interested in HCI: What should the human-agent relationship look like in order to actually maintain reliable oversight? 'Human in the loop' is necessary, but the current process itself makes the loop stultifying and encourages the human to take themselves out of the loop."

I think we need to clearly distinguish between two different meanings of 'AI is a normal technology.' One interpretation suggests that AI is 'intrinsically normal,' meaning its properties are similar to those of other technologies, which seems implausible since we have invested dumb matter with agency and significant autonomy. The other interpretation is that AI is 'extrinsically normal,' subject to the same social, cultural, political, and environmental constraints as other technologies, and should not be expected to dominate history simply because it is considered special.

The second reading is much more plausible and can be tested empirically. It would be helpful if, when people say 'AI clearly isn’t normal tech,' they specify which interpretation they mean. I suspect that @sayashk and @random_walker lean towards the second interpretation, while many who disagree may interpret their stance as supporting the first.

@TheZvi: "Contra Mr. Hunting, my hot take, as I noted writing up the @dwarkesh_sp interview of Jensen, is that even if AI was a normal technology (which it isn't), export controls are still clearly the correct move right now."

I think this is a debate worth having, but here is my main response: everything is a transition period to something else. The only non-transition period is the end of humanity when the sun burns out or something like that. I'm not being facetious here.

If everything is a transition period, then it becomes a question of what is useful to model and think through using the economic toolkit. My take, and some may disagree, is that we should focus on scenarios and conditions built on foundations that we have at least some evidence for, at least some precedent. Otherwise, we are just doing sci-fi, like drugs that may come in the future that change our preferences for socialization; I don't see how this is different from writing a script for a movie like The Fifth Element. All of this is certainly possible, but so are a billion other things. The confidence bands around 'the unprecedented' are so wide that I'm not sure how useful the economic toolkit is for it.

@StefanFSchubert: "There's been a good debate among economists about the long-run impact of AI on the labour market, centred on a nice essay by @alexolegimas. But I think some economists still underestimate how weird the world of advanced AI will be (though not @Jabaluck)."

I appreciate the work by @EpochAIResearch and @GregHBurnham in flagging and fixing these issues. Finding bugs in evaluations is always disappointing, but in the long run, it is necessary and extremely helpful for improving evaluations. It also reminds me of the issues we uncovered in CORE-Bench. As benchmarks become more complex, analyzing benchmark tasks and agent logs will become more important to ensure the validity of evaluation results.

Coincidentally, today we released a paper led by @PKirgis on how to do log analysis well. This builds on all our lessons from the trenches in conducting such evaluations and fixing the issues we found in our own work. I’m sure we’ll find many other issues in our evaluations, but I genuinely think the evaluations community will be better off for having developed tools and methods to improve evaluation rigor.

